Group type: large
Definition: a side. Membership places a character within a larger order or against it: a nation, a faction, an institution, a cause, a criminal organisation. What matters is not how many members it has but that belonging to it positions a character relative to everyone else in the world.
Test: knowing only that a character belongs to this group, could you predict who they would stand with and who they would stand against?
Include: nations and states, factions at war, institutions and the organisations that oppose them, orders, criminal syndicates, movements, causes, corporations, dynasties, faiths.
Exclude: units and teams inside a single side. A class within a school, a squad within an army, a department within a company are not sides — the school, the army and the company are.
Size does not matter. A conspiracy of five that opposes an institution of thousands is still a side, and both are returned.
Non-overlap: a character belongs to at most one side. Where two candidates cover the same people, return the one that better divides the cast.
Coverage: account for the entire cast. Do not invent membership.
